cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this model, credit its author and keep the licence file with it.
title: Mille "Mire" Feuille (free original 3D model)
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: Mohka Noir
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Redistributed under the author's Apache-2.0 licence (LICENSE, readme/Readme-LicenseText-Full(EN).txt).
  The author's summary (readme/Readme-SimpleLicense(EN-JP).txt) allows redistribution, derivative
  versions, re-upload and revenue, on condition that the original source is credited or the licence
  file is included. Mire.blend, MireQuest.blend, Mire.unitypackage and readme/ are the author's
  files, unchanged.


  MODIFIED FILES (Apache-2.0 section 4(b)): usd/Mire.usda is not the author's file. The repository
  maintainers made it from Mire.blend on 2026-09-29 with Blender 5.2.1 LTS (build 9e2066aef7ef,
  factory settings), a local conversion tool, and changed the model on the way, in memory only
  (Mire.blend is unchanged). The changes: (1) On the mesh Body, the shape keys named with the
  symbols U+2227 and U+25B2 are renamed u2227 and u25B2: both become "_" as USD names, and the
  exporter then writes 110 blend shape names but 109 targets, which makes a USD reader drop every
  blend shape of the face. (2) Only the object Armature and its 18 child meshes are exported; Body.LP
  and Mire.Ms.Body.LP (the low-poly version) and Camera are not. The meshes keep the places the
  .blend gives them: the avatar at the origin wears the shirt, spats and shoes, and the other outfit
  pieces (Mire.Ms.Body.A, .B and .C, Mire.Ms.Fleur.Dress, Mire.Ms.Fleur.Dress.Alt, Mire.Ms.Sweater)
  stand 1 to 3 m beside it, bound to the same skeleton.


  EXPORTER NAMING AND SHAPE VALUES: a USD name holds only letters, digits and "_", so the exporter
  spells every other character as "_". This changes 76 of the 117 joint names (Upper_leg.L becomes
  Upper_leg_L) and 25 more blend shape names: 24 on Body, the fifteen viseme shapes among them, and
  "Key 4" on Mire.Ms.Body.B, which becomes Key_4. The exporter also makes the blend shape names the
  skeleton drives unique across the stage: Pants, Top and StockingsOff keep their names on
  Mire.Ms.Arms and take the suffixes 2 to 9 on Mire.Ms.Body.A, .B, .Butt, .C, .Legs, .Shoulders,
  .Thighs and .Torso, in that order (Pants2 to Pants9). The shape key value the .blend sets
  (StockingsOff at 1 on Mire.Ms.Body.Butt, StockingsOff4 in USD) goes to the SkelAnimation
  /Mire/Armature/Armature/Anim, which the skeleton does not bind, so a USD reader shows it at 0: 96
  vertices sit up to 15.5 mm from the .blend's look, under the spats.


  EXPORT OPTIONS (bpy.ops.wm.usd_export, every other option at its default): filepath=Mire.usda,
  check_existing=False, selected_objects_only=True, export_animation=False, export_hair=False,
  export_uvmaps=True, rename_uvmaps=True, export_mesh_colors=True, export_normals=True,
  export_materials=True, export_subdivision=BEST_MATCH, export_armatures=True,
  only_deform_bones=False, export_shapekeys=True, use_instancing=False, evaluation_mode=VIEWPORT,
  generate_preview_surface=True, generate_materialx_network=False, convert_orientation=False,
  export_textures_mode=NEW, overwrite_textures=True, relative_paths=True, xform_op_mode=TRS,
  root_prim_path=/Mire, export_custom_properties=True, author_blender_name=True,
  convert_world_material=False, allow_unicode=True, export_meshes=True, export_lights=False,
  export_cameras=False, export_curves=False, export_points=False, export_volumes=False,
  triangulate_meshes=False, usdz_downscale_size=KEEP, merge_parent_xform=False,
  convert_scene_units=METERS. The stage is Z up, 1 metre per unit, rest pose, no animation.
  usd/textures/ holds the eleven images packed in Mire.blend, written byte for byte as packed.
  Two exports of the same .blend write the same prims and values in a different prim order.


  ADDED FILES: Mire.wardrobe.tsv and Mire.materials.tsv are the repository maintainers' data, not
  the author's. Mire.wardrobe.tsv gives a layer to meshes of the package's Mire.fbx, named as the
  FBX names its nodes: off (an outfit piece that Mire.blend places beside the avatar, so not worn),
  replaced (hidden while a garment drawn with the pen is worn) and over (worn over that garment).
  Mire.materials.tsv gives an image to a material whose own image is in neither the package nor
  usd/textures/: Chroma.Mt.Hair takes Mire.Tx.Hair.png, the image of the package's Mire.Mt.Hair.
